Keep a sending stream's RTP timestamp on real time across a gap in the PCM (Y2Linux).

The encoders advance the RTP timestamp only by the PCM frames they send. The
PCM can stop for a while without the stream stopping: Reborn drops the PCM
between tracks (~50 ms without data per skip), and since the radio daemon
keeps a paused headset's stream started (--keep-alive=900, no AVDTP Suspend)
a pause is a gap of any length. Every later packet then carried a timestamp
that much behind real time, and a sink that plays by the timestamp drops late
packets: on the developer's AirPods Pro the lag added up per skip (-0.054,
-0.102, -0.163 s: crackle after two skips, silence after three) and was -0.98 s
after a pause, measured from btmon RTP headers (2026-11-04). The audio itself
kept flowing at 47 packets/s throughout.

The sender now anchors frame 0 to the clock the encoders pace with
(gettimestamp: CLOCK_MONOTONIC_RAW; the adjusted CLOCK_MONOTONIC drifted from
it by ~1 ms/s after boot and moved the timestamp with no gap) and, when real
time is more than 40 ms ahead of the frames sent, moves the timestamp forward to
real time (never back). Decoding (rtp_state_sync_stream) is unchanged. Patch
0008 refills the sink's buffer after such a gap.

--- a/src/rtp.h
